  "title": "CHAPTER 3: TAMIL NADU POLITY AND ADMINISTRATION",
  "order": 3,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "8bzk7jf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3.1 State Government Structure"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Tamil Nadu is a state in the Indian Union governed according to the Constitution of India. The Governor is the constitutional head of the state and is appointed by the President of India. The Chief Minister heads the Council of Ministers and is the executive head.",
      "id": "9ru2rca"
    },
    {
      "id": "zoe2xm6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Legislature"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Tamil Nadu has a unicameral legislature – the Tamil Nadu Legislative Assembly (Vidhan Sabha). There are 234 constituencies. Members are elected by direct popular vote for a term of 5 years. The Legislative Council (Vidhan Parishad) was abolished in 1986.",
      "id": "cyes578"
    },
    {
      "id": "gc5ioqt",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Total Assembly seats: 234",
        "Lok Sabha seats: 39",
        "Rajya Sabha seats: 18",
        "Reserved SC seats in Assembly: 44",
        "Reserved ST seats in Assembly: 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "8eibsuw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Judiciary"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Madras High Court is one of the oldest High Courts in India, established in 1862. It has jurisdiction over Tamil Nadu and Puducherry. It has a Principal Bench at Chennai and a Madurai Bench established in 2004.",
      "id": "1m3zsnm"
    },
    {
      "id": "vn38eja",
